    model: 'Maslow’s hierarchy suggests that when basic physiological and safety needs such as housing, food and security are unmet, people struggle to pursue higher needs such as belonging, esteem and education, so it is understandable that college has fallen away. Cognitive appraisal theory adds that how he interprets his situation, for example as hopeless or as evidence of personal failure, shapes his emotions; appraising it as permanent may produce despair. Saying he does not care about anything could reflect anhedonia (loss of pleasure) and avolition (loss of motivation), which are symptoms of depression, so he should be screened for depression and suicide risk. Care should begin by connecting him to shelter, food and safety, then using motivational interviewing to explore his own goals, supporting his autonomy and setting small achievable steps, such as attending one class or one appointment, while monitoring his mood.',
