    model: 'Forming a new memory requires encoding the experience, holding it in short-term memory and then consolidating it into long-term storage, a process that depends on the hippocampus. Alzheimer disease damages the hippocampus early, so new experiences such as this morning’s visit are not consolidated and are quickly lost. This is a pattern of anterograde memory loss, the inability to form new memories. Her wedding memory is an episodic long-term memory consolidated decades ago and rehearsed many times; such older memories are stored more widely across the cortex and are relatively preserved early in the disease, although retrograde loss of older memories increases as the disease progresses. Helpful supports include a large calendar and written notes of visits, consistent daily routines, reminder alarms and pill organizers, and keeping familiar photos and objects nearby.',
